7- Stability and Sensitivity of the Recurrence-Aware Trust Model
The recurrence-aware trust-scoring mechanism is evaluated using semi-synthetic sequences of clean and 
reinforcement-learning-manipulated flow windows, including patterns such as one weak window followed
by multiple reliable windows. Although these scenarios provide controlled experiments, they do not necessarily 
reproduce the temporal dependence, burstiness, and behavioral drift of operational IoT traffic. 
Please justify the selected temporal patterns and evaluate the trust model under alternative anomaly frequencies,
burst durations, and nonstationary traffic conditions. Where possible, validation using temporally ordered 
traffic traces would strengthen the evidence that the proposed recurrence mechanism can identify low-rate
manipulation without unnecessarily disrupting legitimate sessions.

8- Limitations of the Semi-Synthetic Temporal Evaluation
The effectiveness of the trust-scoring mechanism that is aware of the recurrence is tested with semi-synthetic sequences
consisting of a sequence of clean flow windows and a sequence of flow windows manipulated using the reinforcement learning
method, including patterns like a single weak flow window followed by several reliable flow windows. While these scenarios
afford controlled experiments, they are not necessarily representative of the temporal dependency, burstiness, and behavioral
drift found in operational IoT traffic. Justify the chosen temporal patterns and analyze the trust model when the anomaly 
frequency, anomaly duration and the nonstationary traffic are changed. In the future, it would be useful to have more evidence
that the proposed recurrence mechanism can detect low-rate manipulation without interfering with legitimate sessions, 
where such evidence can be provided by validation based on temporally ordered traces of traffic.
